Subject: xbla: look two folders deeper for the package inside a dropped archive

the user's own XBLA archive ("Perfect Dark.rar", the copy this port is built
around) stores the STFS package as

    Perfect Dark/584109C2/000D0000/35C1CDD22DD0D4E54B858859C0052124FFFAD17958

which is four names deep. XBLAIMPORT_SCAN_DEPTH is 2 and xblaScanDir()
examines the directory it is given plus `depth` more levels, so it reaches
three names and no further. The .rar IS accepted and IS unpacked correctly -
248 MB into cache/xbla/ - and then:

    ERROR: xbla: no Xbox 360 package inside .../xbla/Perfect Dark.rar

unpackFailed is latched, so nothing of the release ever draws. And because
the .extracted marker is only written after that scan succeeds, and
xblaFindExtractedIn() uses the same depth, every later boot unpacks the same
248 MB again and fails again.

Proven on the clang oracle 2026-09-12: with the unpacked package moved by
hand to cache/xbla/<hash> and .extracted touched, the same binary logs
"xblamesh: 2616 slots", "xblatex: 5747 texture records" and "xblastage:
bgdata/bg_pete.seg from the release", and Chicago draws 2880 triangles at the
comparison frame against 477 with the release off
(artifacts/oracle/baseline/chicago-{n64,xbla}.jpg).

4 rather than 3 so that a player who unpacked the archive by hand INTO xbla/
- which puts the same tree one level lower - is found too. The scan stops at
the first package, and every level it walks is the player's own xbla/ or our
own cache, so depth costs nothing here.

Worth sending upstream: it is a one-line fix for a real archive layout.

NOT YET APPLIED TO A BUILD - scripts/apply-overlay.sh does not exist yet
(Phase 1). The 0.3 oracles are stock upstream; the XBLA verification above
was done by flattening cache/xbla/ by hand.

diff --git a/port/src/xblaimport.c b/port/src/xblaimport.c
index f999704d9..15902a1ca 100644
--- a/port/src/xblaimport.c
+++ b/port/src/xblaimport.c
@@ -53,7 +53,13 @@
 // How far into that folder to look. An archive in the wild wraps the package
 // in a folder of its own ("Perfect Dark/<content id>"), and a player who
 // unpacked one by hand has that folder sitting in xbla/.
-#define XBLAIMPORT_SCAN_DEPTH 2
+//
+// Four, not two: the common .rar wraps it deeper still - "Perfect Dark/<title
+// id>/<type>/<content id>", which is four names - and a copy unpacked by hand
+// into xbla/ puts that whole tree one level lower again. At two the archive
+// unpacks and the package is then not found, which latches unpackFailed and
+// re-unpacks 248MB on every boot.
+#define XBLAIMPORT_SCAN_DEPTH 4
 
 // Where an archive dropped in xbla/ comes apart: cache/xbla/ beside the
 // executable (or in the save directory), so that xbla/ itself holds nothing
